#!/bin/sh
# A CommitLore trailer git will not parse is not a trailer.
#
# Six times on 2026-08-22 a `Limit:` or `Ruled-out:` wrapped onto a second line, which ends the
# trailer block — so the record was written, looked right in the editor, and was stored by nobody.
# CommitLore prints a warning and exits 0, which is a note about a mistake that already exists.
#
# This hook used to hold its own copy of the rule, in shell. That copy and the range check in
# `scripts/verify-trailers-are-parsable.mjs` were two implementations of one rule, and they did not
# agree: the range check could not see a trailer stranded in a paragraph that was not the last one,
# which is what a squash merge produces, and it reported three such merges on `main` as clean while
# 41 records were dropped. One rule has one implementation now, and this hook is one of its callers.
#
# The script asks `git interpret-trailers --parse` rather than restating its rule, because every
# phrasing of the question that is not "run the code that decides" can drift from the answer — and
# the first version of this hook drifted exactly that way.
MSG_FILE="$1"
[ -f "$MSG_FILE" ] || exit 0

ROOT=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null) || exit 0
CHECK="$ROOT/scripts/verify-trailers-are-parsable.mjs"
[ -f "$CHECK" ] || exit 0

# Which interpreter runs the check (#808).
#
# Until #808 this line said `node`, a bare name, so whether the hook checked anything at all
# depended on the PATH of whoever ran `git` — and git does not hand a hook a login shell's PATH. A
# commit from a GUI client, an editor or a launchd job arrives here with no `node` on it, which is
# what CommitLore's own `doctor` reports on this machine today.
#
# The order below runs from "the operator's own answer" to "the answer the calling process is
# already living on", and stops there:
#
#   1. `command -v node` — the PATH git actually gave this hook. If it has one, that is the
#      interpreter the operator arranged for, and no further guessing is warranted.
#   2. `$NODE` / `$npm_node_execpath` — npm and pnpm set both to the absolute path of the
#      interpreter running the current package script. `scripts/merge-pr.mjs` under `pnpm merge`
#      spawns git from inside exactly such a run, so this is not a guess about the host: it names
#      the runtime the caller is already executing on.
#
# Deliberately NOT in the list: well-known host locations (`/opt/homebrew/bin/node`,
# `/usr/local/bin/node`, a version manager's shims). Those are guesses, and a wrong guess is the
# *other* half of this defect — the check would run, silently, on a runtime nobody chose, free to
# disagree with the `engines` range in package.json. Every candidate above was named by the
# operator or by the process that invoked git. None was invented here.
#
# A candidate is ESTABLISHED before its exit status is trusted, and this is the whole of it.
#
# The first version of this resolution accepted `$NODE` on `[ -x "$NODE" ]`. That tests the file,
# not the runtime: `-x` is true of `/usr/bin/true`, of a shim for a runtime that was uninstalled,
# of any wrapper that exits 0 — and the hook's entire verdict about your commit message is that
# file's exit status. So `NODE=/usr/bin/true` made a wrapped `Limit:` pass, which is the one thing
# this hook exists to refuse. Measured: the hook this replaced refused that message (loudly, for
# the wrong reason, which is #808); the `-x` version accepted it, silently, and the record was gone.
# A guard that says yes because something exited 0 is strictly worse than one that says no for the
# wrong reason.
#
# So each candidate is asked a question only a Node interpreter answers, and the answer is read
# rather than the status:
#
#   `-p 'process.versions.node'` evaluates the expression and prints its value. An executable that
#   merely exits 0 prints nothing and fails. An executable that prints something else fails the
#   version shape. Nothing about the commit message is involved, and no exit status is consulted:
#   the check is a property of the interpreter, established before the interpreter is given any
#   authority over the message.
#
# What this does NOT establish: that the executable is trustworthy. Anything that prints a version
# string passes, so an executable built to impersonate node still gets in. That is a PATH-trust
# problem, it is older and wider than this hook, and it is not solved here. `command -v node` is
# consulted first, so an executable named `node` planted early on git's PATH is chosen ahead of
# `$NODE` — and `pnpm` and `npm` prepend `<repo>/node_modules/.bin`, so a change under review could
# in principle ship the interpreter that checks it. The hook this replaced resolved `node` through
# PATH the same way. Narrowed, not closed: measured, a planted `node` that only exits 0 is now
# refused where it used to pass, and one that prints a version string is accepted by both.
#
# Only the first line is read, and it is read with the shell's own `read` rather than `head`. Two
# reasons, both learned here:
#
#   - No external command may be involved. The environment this hook has to survive is one whose
#     PATH is missing things, which is the whole of #808; a probe spelled with `head` reported "not
#     an interpreter" for a perfectly good node whenever `head` was not on the PATH either, and a
#     probe that fails for reasons of its own is a fail-closed with no bottom.
#   - Stopping at the first line bounds a candidate that writes without stopping — BSD `yes` is the
#     easy example — which would otherwise hang every commit instead of answering. Residual: a
#     candidate that writes forever without ever emitting a newline still blocks here.
answers_as_a_node_interpreter() {
  "$1" -p 'process.versions.node' 2>/dev/null | {
    IFS= read -r ANSWER || ANSWER=""
    case "$ANSWER" in
      [0-9]*.[0-9]*.[0-9]*) exit 0 ;;
      *) exit 1 ;;
    esac
  }
}

# The loop falls through rather than stopping at the first candidate that exists: a stale `$NODE`
# left over from another project must not consume the slot `npm_node_execpath` would have filled.
NODE_BIN=""
for CANDIDATE in "$(command -v node 2>/dev/null)" "$NODE" "$npm_node_execpath"; do
  [ -n "$CANDIDATE" ] || continue
  if answers_as_a_node_interpreter "$CANDIDATE"; then
    NODE_BIN="$CANDIDATE"
    break
  fi
done

# "The check refused" and "the check could not run" are different facts, and until #808 they shared
# one exit path and one sentence: a missing interpreter made the check exit 127, `if !` read that as
# a refusal, and the hook told the author their well-formed message writes a record git will not
# store. The stated cause was not the cause.
#
# The choice here is to REFUSE the commit, exit 1, and say why.
#
#   - Exiting 0 would let every commit on a machine with no `node` on git's PATH be stored
#     unchecked, with nothing saying so. That failure is silent, durable and machine-wide: it is not
#     one bad commit, it is a guard that stopped existing while continuing to report success. This
#     repository has already lost record lines to a check that was green over nothing.
#   - Exiting 1 blocks work on a machine that merely lacks a PATH entry. That cost is real, but it
#     is loud, immediate, self-describing, and cleared by putting node on the PATH git uses. It is
#     also the one case where `--no-verify` is a deliberate, visible act rather than a silent gap.
#
# This is a claim about THIS path only, and the file does not follow it everywhere.
#
# An earlier draft of this comment said the two `exit 0` lines above are a different call because a
# missing `$MSG_FILE` or `$CHECK` means "no such rule exists here". That does not survive reading
# line 22: this hook is sitting there calling `$CHECK`, which is the rule existing. A commit that
# deletes `scripts/verify-trailers-are-parsable.mjs` and wraps a `Limit:` is accepted, while a
# machine that merely lacks node is refused. The file was asserting a principle it did not follow.
#
# The missing-`$CHECK` path deliberately chooses the other way, and the reason is that the two are
# different threats rather than the same one seen twice. A deleted checker is a change to the
# repository: it is in the diff, it is in review, it is in `git log`, and it is the same for every
# clone. A missing interpreter is a property of one machine at one moment, invisible to everyone
# else, and it silently disarms the check for whoever has it while the repository still reads as
# guarded. The first is a decision someone made and can be seen to have made; the second is a gap
# nobody chose and nobody can see. Refusing here closes the invisible one.
#
# That is a reason, not a proof that the split is right. If the missing-`$CHECK` path should also
# refuse, that is its own unit of work — deleting the checker is a different threat and widening
# this change to cover it would put two decisions behind one review.
if [ -z "$NODE_BIN" ]; then
  echo "commit-msg: could not run the trailer check — no Node interpreter found." >&2
  echo "  tried, in order: PATH (command -v node), \$NODE, \$npm_node_execpath" >&2
  echo "  Each had to answer \`-p process.versions.node\` with a version. An executable that only" >&2
  echo "  exits 0 is not accepted as an interpreter, because its exit status would then become this" >&2
  echo "  hook's verdict on your message." >&2
  echo "  This says nothing about your commit message. Git does not run hooks with a login" >&2
  echo "  shell's PATH; put node on the PATH git uses, or run git from a \`pnpm\` script." >&2
  exit 1
fi

if ! "$NODE_BIN" "$CHECK" --message-file "$MSG_FILE" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  "$NODE_BIN" "$CHECK" --message-file "$MSG_FILE" >&2
  echo "" >&2
  echo "commit-msg: this message writes a record git will not store." >&2
  exit 1
fi
